Chapter: PitLatch
  cites Foreword chapter Board
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

  grounds Device.Port, Device.Mmio

 The counter-latch command, pinned by the one property that distinguishes it
 from its absence.

 The obvious test -- latch, read both halves, check they agree -- passes
 against a machine with no latch at all. The counter moves between the two
 reads either way, and two bytes sampled a microsecond apart almost always
 look like a coherent pair. A check that passes on a controller that does
 nothing is not a check.

 What a latch actually promises is that the value goes STALE: once frozen,
 it must not follow the counter. So the test reads a coherent value, latches,
 lets a lot of time pass, and then reads the latch. A working latch answers
 the value from before the delay. A missing one answers the value from after
 it -- which is exactly what the live counter says, and that is the tell.

 Channel 2 is used rather than channel 0 because the boot code owns channel 0
 and runs it in mode 3, where the count decrements by two and reloads at half
 the divisor. Channel 2 is programmed here in mode 0 with a divisor of 65536,
 so the count is a plain countdown and the arithmetic below is exact.

Section: Ports

  pit-command : Integer = 67
  pit-ch2 : Integer = 66

 Channel 2, access mode 3 (low byte then high byte), mode 0. The latch
 command is the same channel with an access field of zero, and carries no
 mode bits at all -- writing them would reconfigure the channel on every
 latch.

  pit-config-ch2 : Integer = 176
  pit-latch-ch2 : Integer = 128

Section: Reading

  program-ch2 : [Device.Port] Integer
  program-ch2 =
    let c = port-out-byte pit-command pit-config-ch2
    in let lo = port-out-byte pit-ch2 0
    in let hi = port-out-byte pit-ch2 0
    in c + lo + hi

 A latched read takes the low byte then the high byte; taking both is what
 lifts the latch.

  read-latched : [Device.Port] Integer
  read-latched =
    let l = port-out-byte pit-command pit-latch-ch2
    in let lo = port-in-byte pit-ch2
    in let hi = port-in-byte pit-ch2
    in bit-or lo (bit-shl hi 8)

 Read the frozen value WITHOUT latching first, so the delay sits between the
 latch and the read. This is the call whose answer the whole test turns on.

  read-frozen : [Device.Port] Integer
  read-frozen =
    let lo = port-in-byte pit-ch2
    in let hi = port-in-byte pit-ch2
    in bit-or lo (bit-shl hi 8)

  latch-only : [Device.Port] Integer
  latch-only = port-out-byte pit-command pit-latch-ch2

 The channel counts DOWN and wraps at the divisor, so ticks elapsed between
 two samples is their difference modulo 65536, not their subtraction.

  ticks-between : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ticks-between (earlier) (later) = int-mod (earlier - later + 65536) 65536

 The delay is measured, not counted. A fixed spin of N iterations takes
 whatever wall time the host happens to give it, which put the elapsed count
 either side of the threshold depending on machine load -- a flaky test, and
 a flaky test is worth less than none.

 So channel 1 is the clock. Nothing else uses it, it is programmed in access
 mode 2 so each read returns just the high byte of a live counter -- one byte,
 no pairing problem, no latch involved -- and the high byte advances once per
 256 ticks. Waiting for it to advance a fixed number of steps is a delay in
 TICKS rather than in instructions, so it is the same delay on any host.

  pit-ch1 : Integer = 65
  pit-config-ch1 : Integer = 96

  program-ch1 : [Device.Port] Integer
  program-ch1 =
    let c = port-out-byte pit-command pit-config-ch1
    in c + port-out-byte pit-ch1 0

  ch1-high : [Device.Port] Integer
  ch1-high = port-in-byte pit-ch1

  steps-since : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  steps-since (start) (now) = int-mod (start - now + 256) 256

 Fuel-capped: a channel that never advances ends the wait instead of hanging
 the machine, and the verdict then reports a delay that did not happen.

  wait-steps : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wait-steps (start) (want) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if steps-since start ch1-high >= want then 1
    else wait-steps start want (fuel - 1)

Section: The LAPIC Input Clock

 The local APIC timer divides an input frequency that nothing had ever
 measured or advertised. A guest could rely on the period scaling with the
 initial count and the divisor, and on nothing else -- so a guest calibrating
 the LAPIC against the PIT had no way to know whether the two clocks agreed
 about how fast a second is. This measures it: count LAPIC ticks against PIT
 ticks and work back to the input frequency.

 The timer is armed MASKED, so it counts without delivering an interrupt and
 the boot processor's own PIT-driven scheduling is left alone.

  lapic-base : Integer = #FEE00000
  lapic-lvt-timer : Integer = #320
  lapic-init-count : Integer = #380
  lapic-current-count : Integer = #390
  lapic-divide-cfg : Integer = #3E0

  lapic-divide-by-1 : Integer = 11
  lapic-masked-vector : Integer = #10030

 Twenty milliseconds of period at the nominal rate, which is well clear of
 the one-millisecond floor a period is clamped to.

  lapic-arm-count : Integer = 2000000
  lapic-measure-ticks : Integer = 1000000

  pit-hz : Integer = 1193182

  arm-lapic : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  arm-lapic =
    let d = mmio-write-32 (lapic-base + lapic-divide-cfg) lapic-divide-by-1
    in let v = mmio-write-32 (lapic-base + lapic-lvt-timer) lapic-masked-vector
    in let i = mmio-write-32 (lapic-base + lapic-init-count) lapic-arm-count
    in d + v + i

  lapic-now : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-now = mmio-read-32 (lapic-base + lapic-current-count)

  wait-lapic : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  wait-lapic (start) (want) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if start - lapic-now >= want then 1
    else wait-lapic start want (fuel - 1)

 Work the input frequency back out of the two counts. The PIT high byte
 advances once per 256 ticks, so the elapsed PIT ticks are the steps times
 256, and the LAPIC input rate is its ticks over that same interval.

  implied-hz : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  implied-hz (lapic-ticks) (pit-steps) =
    if pit-steps <= 0 then 0
    else (lapic-ticks * pit-hz) / (pit-steps * 256)

Section: Test

  verdict : Integer, Integer -> Text
  verdict (held) (moved) =
    "latch: held=" & (if held < 3000 then "1" else "0")
      & " moved=" & (if moved > 6000 then "1" else "0")

 Ten percent either side of the nominal hundred megahertz. The point is that
 the number is MEASURED against another clock and lands where the model says
 it should, not that it is exact.

  clock-verdict : Integer -> Text
  clock-verdict (hz) =
    "lapic: near-100mhz=" & (if hz > 90000000 then (if hz < 110000000 then "1" else "0") else "0")

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    let p1 = program-ch1
    in let p = program-ch2
    in let a = read-latched
    in let l0 = latch-only
    in let mark = ch1-high
    in let waited = wait-steps mark 64 60000000
    in let frozen = read-frozen
    in let c = read-latched
    in let armed = arm-lapic
    in let l-start = lapic-now
    in let p-start = ch1-high
    in let lw = wait-lapic l-start lapic-measure-ticks 60000000
    in let p-end = ch1-high
    in let hz = implied-hz lapic-measure-ticks (steps-since p-start p-end)
    in act
      print-line-uni (verdict (ticks-between a frozen) (ticks-between frozen c))
      print-line-uni (clock-verdict hz)
    end
  end
